Hongbin Liu,
Jiebo Luo, 
Zhen Lei<sup id="id1" class="ltx_sup">*</sup>,  

</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Vision-language models (VLMs) such as CLIP and Grounding DINO have achieved remarkable success in object recognition and detection. However, their performance often degrades under real-world distribution shifts. Test-time adaptation (TTA) aims to mitigate this issue by adapting models during inference. Existing methods either rely on computationally expensive backpropagation, which hinders real-time deployment, or focus solely on likelihood adaptation, which overlooks the critical role of the prior.
Our prior work, Bayesian Class Adaptation (BCA), addressed these shortcomings for object recognition by introducing a training-free framework that incorporates adaptive priors.
Building upon this foundation, we now present Bayesian Class Adaptation plus (BCA+), a unified, training-free framework for TTA for both object recognition and detection. BCA+ introduces a dynamic cache that adaptively stores and updates class embeddings, spatial scales (for detection), and, crucially, adaptive class priors derived from historical predictions. We formulate adaptation as a Bayesian inference problem, where final predictions are generated by fusing the initial VLM output with a cache-based prediction. This cache-based prediction combines a dynamically updated likelihood (measuring feature and scale similarity) and a prior (reflecting the evolving class distribution). This dual-adaptation mechanism, coupled with uncertainty-guided fusion, enables BCA+ to correct both the model’s semantic understanding and its contextual confidence. As a training-free method requiring no backpropagation, BCA+ is highly efficient. Extensive experiments demonstrate that BCA+ achieves state-of-the-art performance on both recognition and detection benchmarks.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Index Terms: </h6>Test-Time Adaption, Vision-Language Models, Bayesian Estimation, Training-Free, Object Recognition, Object Detection.

</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">I </span><span id="S1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Introduction</span></h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The ability to recognize and localize objects in images is a fundamental tasks in computer vision <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>. Recent advances in vision-language models (VLMs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>, such as CLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> and Grounding DINO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>, have revolutionized these tasks by leveraging large-scale image-text pairs to learn robust, multi-modal representations. These models achieve remarkable zero-shot performance, enabling object recognition and detection without the need to train a dedicated baseline model from scratch for each new task.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.02750v1/fig1.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:191/143;" width="191" height="143" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 1: </span>Illustrative example of Fixed Prior <span id="S1.F1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">vs.</span> Adaptive Prior: Comparison of Diagnosis Outcomes. In the fixed prior scenario, patients with fever are consistently diagnosed with the common cold, regardless of whether it is a normal period or a COVID-19 period. In contrast, the adaptive prior scenario adjusts the diagnosis based on the current context. During normal periods, patients with fever are diagnosed with the common cold, while during the COVID-19 period, they are more likely to be diagnosed with COVID-19. This demonstrates the importance of performing prior adaptation in different environments.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Despite their impressive capabilities, VLMs are often brittle when deployed in real-world scenarios <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>. This brittleness stems primarily from the distribution shift between the training and test data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>. While VLMs are pre-trained on large-scale, general-purpose datasets, real-world test data often comes from specific domains with distinct characteristics, such as varying environmental conditions, object appearances, or background clutter <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>. This discrepancy between the broad, general distribution of the pre-training data and the narrow, domain-specific distribution of the test data leads to significant performance degradation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>.
To address this challenge, the paradigm of Test-Time Adaptation (TTA) has emerged <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>. TTA aims to adapt pre-trained models online to the test data distribution during inference, without requiring access to the original training data or labels. By continuously updating the model based on incoming test samples, TTA enhances the model’s robustness and adaptability to new environments. Crucially, an effective TTA method must not only improve accuracy but also preserve inference efficiency, ensuring that the adaptation process is fast and lightweight enough for real-time deployment.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For object recognition, numerous methods based on VLMs like CLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> have been proposed <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> and can be broadly divided into two categories. The first relies on fine-tuning text prompts via backpropagation to minimize prediction entropy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>. While effective, this approach is computationally expensive and incompatible with the real-time requirement of TTA. The second category adopts a training-free strategy, using a memory bank to store historical visual embeddings and updating class embeddings statistically <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>. These methods enable efficient, real-time adaptation. However, from a Bayesian perspective, these methods focus solely on likelihood adaptation, updating class embeddings, while neglecting the prior, which reflects the model’s belief about the current class distribution. Since the posterior depends on both likelihood and prior, ignoring prior adaptation limits robustness, especially when the test-time class distribution shifts significantly. We illustrate the importance of prior adaptation using an example in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Fig. 1 ‣ I Introduction ‣ Bayesian Test-time Adaptation for Object Recognition and Detection with Vision-language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, showing how the optimal class distribution varies drastically across different test environments.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.02750v1/fig_2.png" id="S1.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:238/73;" width="238" height="73" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 2: </span>Traditional test-time detection <span id="S1.F2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">vs.</span> VLM-based test-time detection. Traditional test-time detection requires training a dedicated detector (e.g., Faster R-CNN) for each specific domain. For example, a model trained on the ”Cat” domain cannot be directly applied to the ”Dog” domain and must be retrained from scratch, which is resource-intensive. Even during test-time adaptation, these methods rely on backpropagation for updates. While VLM-based method uses a single VLM (e.g., Grounding DINO) as a universal baseline, enabling detection for any category (e.g., both ”Cat” and ”Dog”) without retraining. Our BCA+ framework enables training-free adaptation via a dynamic cache, eliminating backpropagation for real-time deployment.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In contrast, for object detection, the landscape is markedly different. Despite the success of VLMs in TTA for recognition, there is a striking lack of work on TTA for object detection using VLMs like Grounding DINO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>. Existing TTA methods for detection <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite> still use traditional detectors like Faster R-CNN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>. A major drawback of these methods is that they require training a new model from scratch for each specific task or domain. This makes them very costly in terms of time and resources. In contrast, VLMs like Grounding DINO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> can naturally handle open-vocabulary detection, meaning they can detect objects from any category defined by text, without needing to retrain a new model. This makes them a much more flexible and scalable choice. Moreover, these existing TTA methods for detection usually update the model during testing by using backpropagation. This process is very slow and takes a long time, which goes against the main goal of TTA: to adapt quickly and efficiently in real time. Therefore, there is a strong need for a new TTA method that is both training-free and can work effectively with powerful VLMs like Grounding DINO for the object detection task.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To address these challenges, we propose Bayesian Class Adaptation plus (BCA+), a unified, training-free framework for test-time adaptation in both object recognition and detection.
The process unfolds in three key stages. First, for each incoming image, the pre-trained VLM generates an initial prediction along with its visual features (and bounding box for detection). These initial predictions can be misled by distribution shifts. Second, to correct these predictions, BCA+ employs a dynamic cache that stores historical information. We formulate a cache-based prediction by treating the adaptation process as a Bayesian inference problem. This cache-based prediction is derived by combining a likelihood, which measures how similar the current image is to previously seen patterns (based on visual features and spatial scales), with a dynamically updated prior, which captures the belief about which categories are more likely to appear in the current environment. Third, BCA+ performs an uncertainty-guided fusion to produce the final prediction. This fusion intelligently combines the initial VLM prediction and the cache-based prediction, giving more weight to the one that is more confident. Finally, the cache itself is continuously updated: high-confidence final predictions are used to either create new entries for novel patterns or to incrementally refine existing entries in the cache, ensuring the model’s knowledge grows and adapts over time. This entire process is training-free, enabling BCA+ to achieve robust and accurate predictions for both tasks while maintaining high inference efficiency.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">BCA+ extends our prior framework, Bayesian Class Adaptation (BCA) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>, in three key areas: research scope, methodology, and evaluation. Conceptually, BCA+ broadens its application from exclusively object recognition to a unified framework that seamlessly handles both recognition and the more complex task of object detection. Methodologically, we introduce a dynamic cache that adaptively manages historical information, including class embeddings, spatial scales, and priors, departing from BCA’s fixed cache. This is complemented by a new dual-stream prediction framework and an uncertainty-guided fusion strategy that intelligently combines predictions from both the pre-trained VLM and our dynamic cache, leading to more robust results. Empirically, we provide a more comprehensive validation, including extensive new benchmarks for object detection and in-depth analyses, demonstrating that BCA+ achieves state-of-the-art performance with high efficiency.
Our key contributions are as follows:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We present BCA+, a unified, training-free TTA framework that effectively handles both object recognition and object detection. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first work to apply TTA to object detection using VLMs.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce a dynamic cache that simultaneously adapts both the likelihood (class embeddings and spatial scales for detection) and the prior (class distribution). This dual-adaptation mechanism provides a more comprehensive and robust solution to handle distribution shifts.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce a novel dual-stream prediction framework and an uncertainty-guided fusion strategy. This methodological advance, which is not present in BCA, intelligently combines predictions from the pre-trained VLM and our dynamic cache, leading to more robust results.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We demonstrate through extensive experiments that BCA+ achieves state-of-the-art performance on standard benchmarks for both tasks, while maintaining high inference efficiency suitable for real-time applications.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">II </span><span id="S2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Related Work</span></h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">II-A</span> </span><span id="S2.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Vision-Language Models</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Traditional computer vision systems often require training a dedicated deep neural network for each specific task, a process that is both labor-intensive and lacks generalization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>. This paradigm has been challenged by the emergence of VLMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>. By learning multi-modal representations from massive web-scale image-text pairs, VLMs can perform a wide range of visual tasks with a single, pre-trained model, enabling zero-shot and open-vocabulary capabilities.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A seminal work in this field is CLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>, which uses contrastive learning to align image and text embeddings in a shared semantic space. This alignment allows CLIP to perform zero-shot object recognition: given a set of class names, the model can classify an image by comparing its visual embedding to the text embeddings of the class names, without any task-specific fine-tuning. The success of CLIP has inspired a wave of follow-up research. ALIGN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite> demonstrated the power of scale by training on over a billion noisy image-text pairs. FILIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite> introduced a fine-grained interaction mechanism to better capture local semantic alignments between images and text. PaLI <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite> explored joint scaling of both the vision and language components, pushing the boundaries of model size and capability. ZeroVL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite> focuses on data-efficient pre-training by introducing a novel data augmentation strategy into the contrastive learning framework. LiT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite> proposes contrastive tuning with a locked image encoder, fine-tuning only a lightweight text-side adapter for efficient knowledge transfer.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The success of VLMs in object recognition has inspired a range of approaches to extend their capabilities to the more complex task of object detection. This has led to two main research directions: knowledge distillation and end-to-end open-vocabulary detectors.
The first direction focuses on distilling knowledge from powerful VLMs like CLIP into existing detector frameworks.
ViLD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite> is a seminal work in this area, which distills the image-level knowledge from a CLIP teacher into a two-stage detector, enabling it to detect objects from arbitrary categories. Another approach leverages VLMs to generate pseudo-labels for training.
DetCLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite> uses a large-scale image captioning dataset to generate pseudo bounding box labels, effectively expanding the detector’s knowledge base and generalization ability.
The second, and more recent, direction aims to build end-to-end open-vocabulary detectors that natively integrate VLMs.
OV-DETR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite> uses image and text embeddings from a CLIP model as learnable queries within the DETR framework <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>, allowing it to decode category-specified boxes directly from language inputs.
GLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite> formulates object detection as a visual grounding problem, leveraging additional phrase-grounding data to learn aligned semantics at both the phrase and region levels. This formulation has proven so effective that GLIP can even surpass traditional detectors on fully-supervised benchmarks. Building upon these ideas, Grounding DINO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> represents a major leap forward by integrating a language-guided query selection mechanism and a feature enhancement module, achieving state-of-the-art performance in open-vocabulary detection.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Our work builds upon these powerful VLMs, using CLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> for object recognition and Grounding DINO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> for object detection as our base models.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">II-B</span> </span><span id="S2.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Test-Time Adaptation</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Test-Time Adaptation aims to improve the performance of a pre-trained model on distribution-shifted test data by adapting it online, without access to the original training data or labels. Early TTA methods were primarily developed for standard deep neural networks and can be applied to both object recognition and object detection. For recognition, common strategies included entropy minimization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>, <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>, recalibration of batch normalization statistics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>, <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite>, pseudo-labeling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>, <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite> and consistency regularization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>, <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite>. For object detection, methods like STFAR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite> mitigated the impact of noisy pseudo-labels through feature distribution regularization, while MemCLR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite> enhanced target-specific representations using contrastive learning in dynamic environments. Some approaches <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite> also developed online frameworks with dynamic parameter optimization to handle continuous domain shifts. While these early methods demonstrated effectiveness, they are often tightly coupled to specific model architectures and require careful hyperparameter tuning. More importantly, they typically rely on backpropagation for adaptation, which is computationally expensive and hinders real-time deployment. These limitations highlight the need for a more flexible and efficient adaptation paradigm.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The advent of powerful pre-trained VLMs like CLIP has opened a new frontier for TTA. These models, with their inherent zero-shot capabilities, provide a flexible and generalizable foundation for adaptation. Benefiting from the success of CLIP, a growing body of work has focused on test-time adaptation for object recognition. Early approaches like TPT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> optimize text prompts by minimizing prediction entropy across augmented views, while DiffTPT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> and C-TPT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite> enhance this process with better data augmentation and prompt calibration. PromptAlign <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite> aligns the statistics of test samples with those of the source domain by matching their token distribution, thereby optimizing the text prompt for improved generalization.
HisTPT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite> introduces multiple knowledge banks to maintain a comprehensive memory of past data, effectively mitigating catastrophic forgetting during test-time adaptation.
However, these methods rely on backpropagation during testing, which is computationally costly.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To achieve real-time adaptation, a new line of research has emerged, focusing on training-free methods that avoid backpropagation. These approaches adapt the model using only forward passes and simple updates. TDA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> uses a lightweight cache to progressively refine pseudo-labels. DOTA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> continually estimates the distribution of test samples. MTA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>]</cite> manages augmented views in a training-free manner. Zero <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite> proposes a simple yet effective strategy by setting the Softmax temperature to zero, converting soft probability distributions into hard one-hot encodings to guide model adaptation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Despite all this progress in recognition,
there are very few methods that apply TTA to open-vocabulary object detection using VLMs like Grounding DINO. Most existing TTA methods for detection are built on older, closed-set models like Faster RCNN and still rely on slow, gradient-based updates. To fill this gap, we propose BCA+, a unified, training-free framework that can adapt both CLIP for recognition and Grounding DINO for detection in real time.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">III </span><span id="S3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Preliminaries</span></h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CLIP.</span> CLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> is a vision-language model designed for zero-shot object recognition tasks, leveraging large-scale paired image-text data to learn robust, multi-modal representations. Trained via contrastive learning, CLIP aligns images and their corresponding textual descriptions in a shared embedding space, enabling generalization across diverse visual recognition tasks without task-specific fine-tuning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">CLIP comprises two main components: a visual encoder <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., ResNet-50 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> or ViT-B/16 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>]</cite>) and a text encoder <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., a Transformer-based model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>]</cite>). For a <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>-class recognition task, CLIP processes a set of <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> hand-crafted text prompts, such as “a photo of [class <math id="S3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>]” for <math id="S3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1,\dots,K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1,\dots,K</annotation></semantics></math>. These prompts are fed into the text encoder <math id="S3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, which generates normalized text embeddings <math id="S3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{F}_{t}=\{\bm{f}^{t}_{k}\}_{k=1}^{K}\in\mathbb{R}^{K\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑭</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{F}_{t}=\{\bm{f}^{t}_{k}\}_{k=1}^{K}\in\mathbb{R}^{K\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is the embedding dimension. For an input test image <math id="S3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, the visual encoder <math id="S3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> extracts a normalized visual embedding <math id="S3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{f}^{v}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>v</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{f}^{v}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, capturing the semantic content of the image.
To compute predictions, CLIP measures the cosine similarity between the visual embedding <math id="S3.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{f}^{v}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>v</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{f}^{v}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and each text embedding <math id="S3.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{f}^{t}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{f}^{t}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. The probability distribution over the <math id="S3.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> classes is then calculated as:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{clip}(Y=k|\bm{x}_{i})=\frac{\text{exp}(\cos(\bm{f}_{i}^{v},\bm{f}_{k}^{t})))}{\sum_{j=1}^{K}\text{exp}(\cos(\bm{f}_{i}^{v},\bm{f}_{j}^{t})))}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><mtext>exp</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>v</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup><mrow><mtext>exp</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>v</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{clip}(Y=k|\bm{x}_{i})=\frac{\text{exp}(\cos(\bm{f}_{i}^{v},\bm{f}_{k}^{t})))}{\sum_{j=1}^{K}\text{exp}(\cos(\bm{f}_{i}^{v},\bm{f}_{j}^{t})))},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cos(\cdot,\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cos(\cdot,\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the cosine similarity, and <math id="S3.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{exp}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>exp</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{exp}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the exponential function.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Grounding DINO.</span> Grounding DINO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> is a state-of-the-art vision-language model pivotal for open-vocabulary object detection, leveraging large-scale image-text pairs to align visual and textual modalities in a shared embedding space <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>. Trained with detection and grounding objectives, it enables robust detection across arbitrary categories specified by text prompts, serving as a powerful backbone for test-time adaptive object detection.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Grounding DINO employs a dual-encoder architecture comprising a visual encoder <math id="S3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., Swin Transformer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite>) and a text encoder <math id="S3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., BERT-based <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>]</cite>). For an input image <math id="S3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and a set of text prompts <math id="S3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{T}=\{[t_{k}]\}_{k=1}^{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐓</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{T}=\{[t_{k}]\}_{k=1}^{K}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[t_{k}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[t_{k}]</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the <math id="S3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-th category (e.g., “cat . dog .”), the text encoder <math id="S3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> processes <math id="S3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐓</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{T}</annotation></semantics></math> to produce vanilla text embeddings <math id="S3.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{F}_{t}^{\prime}=E_{t}(\mathbf{T})\in\mathbb{R}^{K\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝑭</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐓</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{F}_{t}^{\prime}=E_{t}(\mathbf{T})\in\mathbb{R}^{K\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>. The visual encoder <math id="S3.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> extracts vanilla vision features <math id="S3.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{F}_{v}^{\prime}=E_{v}(\bm{x}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝑭</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{F}_{v}^{\prime}=E_{v}(\bm{x}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, which are then fused with <math id="S3.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{F}_{t}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝑭</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{F}_{t}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> via an attention-based feature enhancer, yielding enhanced visual features <math id="S3.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{F}_{v}=\psi(\bm{F}_{v}^{\prime},\bm{F}_{t}^{\prime})\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{I}\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑭</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝑭</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝑭</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{F}_{v}=\psi(\bm{F}_{v}^{\prime},\bm{F}_{t}^{\prime})\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{I}\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> and enhanced text features <math id="S3.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{F}_{t}=\psi(\bm{F}_{t}^{\prime},\bm{F}_{v}^{\prime})=\{\bm{f}^{t}_{k}\}_{k=1}^{K}\in\mathbb{R}^{K\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑭</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝑭</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝑭</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{F}_{t}=\psi(\bm{F}_{t}^{\prime},\bm{F}_{v}^{\prime})=\{\bm{f}^{t}_{k}\}_{k=1}^{K}\in\mathbb{R}^{K\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{I}</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of image tokens.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To generate detection outputs, Grounding DINO selects <math id="S3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=900" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>900</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=900</annotation></semantics></math> cross-modality queries <math id="S3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\bm{f}_{ij}^{\prime}\}_{j=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒇</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\bm{f}_{ij}^{\prime}\}_{j=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{F}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑭</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{F}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> using a language-guided query selection mechanism based on similarity with <math id="S3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{F}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑭</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{F}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. These queries are refined through a DETR-like <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>]</cite> cross-modality decoder, producing updated query features <math id="S3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\bm{f}_{ij}^{v}\}_{j=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒇</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>v</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\bm{f}_{ij}^{v}\}_{j=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, where each <math id="S3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{f}_{ij}^{v}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒇</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>v</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{f}_{ij}^{v}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> serves as a proposal feature. Then class probabilities for each proposal are computed as:</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{gdino}(Y=k|\bm{x}_{i})=\frac{\text{exp}(\sigma(\cos(\bm{f}_{ij}^{v},\bm{f}_{k}^{t}))))}{\sum_{l=1}^{K}\text{exp}(\sigma(\cos(\bm{f}_{ij}^{v},\bm{f}_{l}^{t}))))}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><mtext>exp</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒇</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>v</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo lspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup><mrow><mtext>exp</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒇</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>v</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{gdino}(Y=k|\bm{x}_{i})=\frac{\text{exp}(\sigma(\cos(\bm{f}_{ij}^{v},\bm{f}_{k}^{t}))))}{\sum_{l=1}^{K}\text{exp}(\sigma(\cos(\bm{f}_{ij}^{v},\bm{f}_{l}^{t}))))},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p5.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(Y|\bm{x}_{ij})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(Y|\bm{x}_{ij})</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the prediction of <math id="S3.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>-th proposal of <math id="S3.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is the sigmoid function. For bounding box prediction, the decoder iteratively refines positional information across its layers, and a regression head processes the final refined proposal feature, combining it with the previous layer’s prediction, to produce coordinates:</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{b}_{ij}=\bm{b}_{ij}^{-1}+\rho(\bm{f}_{ij}^{v})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒃</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒇</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>v</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{b}_{ij}=\bm{b}_{ij}^{-1}+\rho(\bm{f}_{ij}^{v}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p5.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{b}_{ij}\in\mathbb{R}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{b}_{ij}\in\mathbb{R}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the bounding box <math id="S3.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[x,y,w,h]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[x,y,w,h]</annotation></semantics></math> with center coordinates <math id="S3.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x,y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x,y</annotation></semantics></math> and width-height <math id="S3.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w,h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w,h</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{b}_{ij}^{-1}\in\mathbb{R}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒃</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{b}_{ij}^{-1}\in\mathbb{R}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> is the bounding box prediction from the previous decoder layer, and <math id="S3.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> is a regression head applied to the final <math id="S3.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{f}_{ij}^{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝒇</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>v</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{f}_{ij}^{v}</annotation></semantics></math> after iterative refinement.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">IV </span><span id="S4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Method</span></h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem Setting.</span>
In this work, we address the challenge of test-time adaptation for pre-trained vision-language models, focusing on both object recognition and detection. Given a sequence of test images <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\bm{x}_{i}\}_{i=1}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\bm{x}_{i}\}_{i=1}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> that arrive sequentially, our goal is to adapt a pre-trained model to predict accurate outputs for each image <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> immediately in an online manner, despite potential distribution shifts between the pre-training data and test data. For object recognition, we leverage CLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> to generate initial predictions for <math id="S4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> predefined categories. For object detection, we employ Grounding DINO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> to predict a set of bounding boxes and corresponding initial class probabilities among <math id="S4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> predefined categories. While these models provide strong initial predictions, their performance can still degrade under real-world distribution shifts. To address this issue, our method introduces a Bayesian framework that leverages a dynamically updated cache to refine the initial predictions, thereby improving the model’s robustness and accuracy, while preserving inference efficiency for real-time applications.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Notation Unification.</span>
To unify the notation for both tasks, we treat the recognition task as a special case of detection where there is only one proposal per image. Specifically, the visual embedding <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{f}_{i}^{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>v</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{f}_{i}^{v}</annotation></semantics></math> of CLIP is redefined as <math id="S4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{f}_{ij}^{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝒇</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>v</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{f}_{ij}^{v}</annotation></semantics></math> (where <math id="S4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j=1</annotation></semantics></math>) to align with the detection task’s notation. Formally, when an image <math id="S4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is processed by the vision-language model, the outputs can be represented as follows: for object recognition, we obtain <math id="S4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{(\bm{f}_{ij}^{v},\bm{p}^{init}_{ij})\}_{j=1}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒇</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>v</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒑</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(\bm{f}_{ij}^{v},\bm{p}^{init}_{ij})\}_{j=1}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{f}_{ij}^{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝒇</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>v</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{f}_{ij}^{v}</annotation></semantics></math> is the visual embedding of the single proposal and <math id="S4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{p}^{init}_{ij}=P_{clip}(Y|\bm{x}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒑</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{p}^{init}_{ij}=P_{clip}(Y|\bm{x}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> is the initial class probability computed using Eq. (<a href="#S3.E1" title="In III Preliminaries ‣ Bayesian Test-time Adaptation for Object Recognition and Detection with Vision-language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>); for object detection, we obtain <math id="S4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{(\bm{f}_{ij}^{v},\bm{b}_{ij},\bm{p}^{init}_{ij})\}_{j=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒇</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>v</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒑</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(\bm{f}_{ij}^{v},\bm{b}_{ij},\bm{p}^{init}_{ij})\}_{j=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{f}_{ij}^{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝒇</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>v</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{f}_{ij}^{v}</annotation></semantics></math> is the visual embedding of the <math id="S4.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>-th proposal, <math id="S4.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{b}_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{b}_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> is the predicted bounding box, and <math id="S4.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{p}^{init}_{ij}=P_{gdino}(Y|\bm{x}_{ij})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒑</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{p}^{init}_{ij}=P_{gdino}(Y|\bm{x}_{ij})</annotation></semantics></math> is the initial class probability computed using Eq. (<a href="#S3.E2" title="In III Preliminaries ‣ Bayesian Test-time Adaptation for Object Recognition and Detection with Vision-language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.02750v1/fig_3_2.png" id="S4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/175;" width="476" height="175" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Fig. 3: </span>Overview of the proposed BCA+ framework. The process is divided into three main stages: (a) <span id="S4.F3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">VLM Inference</span>, where the pre-trained vision-language model (CLIP for recognition or Grounding DINO for detection) processes the input image <math id="S4.F3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to generate initial outputs, including visual embeddings <math id="S4.F3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\bm{f}_{ij}^{v}\}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒇</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>v</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\bm{f}_{ij}^{v}\}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, initial class predictions <math id="S4.F3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\bm{p}^{init}_{ij}\}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒑</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\bm{p}^{init}_{ij}\}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, and bounding boxes <math id="S4.F3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\bm{b}_{ij}\}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\bm{b}_{ij}\}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> (for detection). (b) <span id="S4.F3.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Bayesian Inference</span>, which computes a cache-based prediction <math id="S4.F3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\bm{p}^{cache}_{ij}\}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒑</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\bm{p}^{cache}_{ij}\}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> by combining a likelihood (derived from feature and scale similarity with cached entries) and a dynamically updated prior. The final prediction <math id="S4.F3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\bm{p}^{final}_{ij}\}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒑</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\bm{p}^{final}_{ij}\}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is produced by fusing the initial and cache-based predictions with an uncertainty-guided strategy. (c) <span id="S4.F3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Cache Adaptation</span>, where the cache is updated based on the final prediction: likelihood adaptation refines the cached feature embeddings <math id="S4.F3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\bm{f}^{cache}_{m}\}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\bm{f}^{cache}_{m}\}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> and scales <math id="S4.F3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\bm{b}^{cache}_{m}\}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\bm{b}^{cache}_{m}\}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, while prior adaptation updates the cached priors <math id="S4.F3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\bm{v}^{cache}_{m}\}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒗</mi><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\bm{v}^{cache}_{m}\}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>. Orange lines denote components exclusive to object recognition, red lines denote components exclusive to object detection, and black lines denote components shared by both tasks.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Overview.</span>
Our proposed Bayesian Class Adaptation plus (BCA+) is a training-free method for test-time adaptation in both object recognition and detection tasks, extending the original BCA framework to handle distribution shifts robustly during inference. As illustrated in Fig. <a href="#S4.F3" title="Fig. 3 ‣ IV Method ‣ Bayesian Test-time Adaptation for Object Recognition and Detection with Vision-language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, the BCA+ framework operates in a closed-loop process consisting of three key stages.
(a) VLM Inference: An input image <math id="S4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is first processed by a pre-trained vision-language model (CLIP for recognition, Grounding DINO for detection). This generates the initial outputs: visual embeddings <math id="S4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\bm{f}_{ij}^{v}\}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒇</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>v</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\bm{f}_{ij}^{v}\}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, initial class predictions <math id="S4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\bm{p}^{init}_{ij}\}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒑</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\bm{p}^{init}_{ij}\}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, and bounding boxes <math id="S4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\bm{b}_{ij}\}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\bm{b}_{ij}\}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> (for detection).
(b) Bayesian Inference: A cache-based prediction <math id="S4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\bm{p}^{cache}_{ij}\}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒑</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\bm{p}^{cache}_{ij}\}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is computed by formulating the adaptation as a Bayesian inference problem. This involves calculating a likelihood based on the similarity between the current proposal’s features (and scales for detection) and those stored in the cache, and combining it with a dynamically updated prior. The final prediction <math id="S4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\bm{p}^{final}_{ij}\}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒑</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\bm{p}^{final}_{ij}\}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is then obtained by fusing the initial prediction <math id="S4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\bm{p}^{init}_{ij}\}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒑</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\bm{p}^{init}_{ij}\}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> and the cache-based prediction <math id="S4.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\bm{p}^{cache}_{ij}\}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒑</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\bm{p}^{cache}_{ij}\}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> using an uncertainty-guided fusion strategy.
(c) Cache Adaptation: The cache is dynamically updated based on high-confidence final predictions. This update has two components: <span id="S4.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">likelihood adaptation</span>, which refines the cached feature embeddings <math id="S4.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\bm{f}^{cache}_{m}\}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\bm{f}^{cache}_{m}\}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> and scales <math id="S4.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\bm{b}^{cache}_{m}\}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\bm{b}^{cache}_{m}\}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> using statistical aggregation, and <span id="S4.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">prior adaptation</span>, which updates the cached priors <math id="S4.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\bm{v}^{cache}_{m}\}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒗</mi><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\bm{v}^{cache}_{m}\}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> to reflect the current test-time class distribution.
By iteratively performing these three stages for each incoming test sample, BCA+ achieves robust and accurate predictions for both tasks under distribution shifts.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">IV-A</span> </span><span id="S4.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Bayesian Inference for Cache-Based Predictions</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As illustrated in the Bayesian Inference stage of Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Fig. 3 ‣ IV Method ‣ Bayesian Test-time Adaptation for Object Recognition and Detection with Vision-language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, our method refines the initial predictions <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\bm{p}^{init}_{ij}\}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒑</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\bm{p}^{init}_{ij}\}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> from the pre-trained VLM (detailed in Section <a href="#S3" title="III Preliminaries ‣ Bayesian Test-time Adaptation for Object Recognition and Detection with Vision-language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III</span></a>) by leveraging a dynamic cache. This section details the core of our framework: how we formulate this refinement as a Bayesian inference problem to compute a cache-based prediction <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\bm{p}^{cache}_{ij}\}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒑</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\bm{p}^{cache}_{ij}\}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS1.6" class="ltx_text">IV-A</span>1 </span><span id="S4.SS1.SSS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Analysis</span></h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.4" class="ltx_p">In this section, we analyze how our method generates cache-based predictions <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\bm{p}^{cache}_{ij}\}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒑</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\bm{p}^{cache}_{ij}\}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> based on cached historical information <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\bm{\mu}_{m}\}_{m=1}^{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\bm{\mu}_{m}\}_{m=1}^{M}</annotation></semantics></math> for both object recognition and object detection tasks. For recognition, the input is a visual embedding <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}_{ij}=(\bm{f}_{ij}^{v})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒇</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>v</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{ij}=(\bm{f}_{ij}^{v})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mu}_{m}=(\bm{f}_{m}^{cache})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mu}_{m}=(\bm{f}_{m}^{cache})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{f}_{m}^{cache}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{f}_{m}^{cache}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the averaged class embedding derived from historical visual embedding. For detection, the input consists of high-confidence proposals <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}_{ij}=(\bm{f}_{ij}^{v},\bm{b}_{ij})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒇</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>v</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{ij}=(\bm{f}_{ij}^{v},\bm{b}_{ij})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mu}_{m}=(\bm{f}_{m}^{cache},\bm{b}_{m}^{cache})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mu}_{m}=(\bm{f}_{m}^{cache},\bm{b}_{m}^{cache})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{b}_{m}^{cache}\in\mathbb{R}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{b}_{m}^{cache}\in\mathbb{R}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> stores the average spatial scale <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[w,h]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[w,h]</annotation></semantics></math>.
Importantly, the number of cached entries <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is not fixed and can be dynamically expanded during inference. This flexibility allows our model to incorporate new knowledge on-the-fly, such as storing additional information in response to novel objects or spatial patterns observed during test time. By leveraging the cached historical information <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\bm{\mu}_{m}\}_{m=1}^{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\bm{\mu}_{m}\}_{m=1}^{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, we compute the posterior probability of class label <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> given input <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> as follows:</p>
<table id="S4.E4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S4.E4X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E4X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P(Y|\bm{x}_{ij})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P(Y|\bm{x}_{ij})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E4X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sum_{m=1}^{M}P(Y,\bm{\mu}_{m}|\bm{x}_{ij})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sum_{m=1}^{M}P(Y,\bm{\mu}_{m}|\bm{x}_{ij})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="3" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E4Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E4Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sum_{m=1}^{M}P(\bm{\mu}_{m}|\bm{x}_{ij})*P(Y|\bm{x}_{ij},\bm{\mu}_{m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sum_{m=1}^{M}P(\bm{\mu}_{m}|\bm{x}_{ij})*P(Y|\bm{x}_{ij},\bm{\mu}_{m})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E4Xb" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E4Xb.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sum_{m=1}^{M}P(\bm{\mu}_{m}|\bm{x}_{ij})*P(Y|\bm{\mu}_{m})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sum_{m=1}^{M}P(\bm{\mu}_{m}|\bm{x}_{ij})*P(Y|\bm{\mu}_{m}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.5" class="ltx_p">The derivation of the posterior probability follows a standard Bayesian treatment. The first line in Eq. (<a href="#S4.E4" title="In IV-A1 Analysis ‣ IV-A Bayesian Inference for Cache-Based Predictions ‣ IV Method ‣ Bayesian Test-time Adaptation for Object Recognition and Detection with Vision-language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) is an application of the law of total probability, summing over all <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mu}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mu}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>. The second line applies the definition of conditional probability. The third line introduces a key assumption: once <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mu}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mu}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> is known, the input <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> provides no additional information about the class label <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>]</cite>. Further, applying Bayesian theorem, the posterior probability <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(\bm{\mu}_{m}|\bm{x}_{ij})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(\bm{\mu}_{m}|\bm{x}_{ij})</annotation></semantics></math> can be further written as:</p>
<table id="S4.E5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S4.E5X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E5X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P(\bm{\mu}_{m}|\bm{x}_{ij})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P(\bm{\mu}_{m}|\bm{x}_{ij})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E5X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{P(\bm{x}_{ij}|\bm{\mu}_{m})*P(\bm{\mu}_{m})}{P(\bm{x}_{ij})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{P(\bm{x}_{ij}|\bm{\mu}_{m})*P(\bm{\mu}_{m})}{P(\bm{x}_{ij})}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="3" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E5Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E5Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{P(\bm{x}_{ij}|\bm{\mu}_{m})*P(\bm{\mu}_{m})}{\sum_{j=1}^{M}P(\bm{x}_{ij},\bm{\mu}_{j})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{P(\bm{x}_{ij}|\bm{\mu}_{m})*P(\bm{\mu}_{m})}{\sum_{j=1}^{M}P(\bm{x}_{ij},\bm{\mu}_{j})}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E5Xb" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E5Xb.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{P(\bm{x}_{ij}|\bm{\mu}_{m})*P(\bm{\mu}_{m})}{\sum_{j=1}^{M}P(\bm{x}_{ij}|\bm{\mu}_{j})*P(\bm{\mu}_{j})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{P(\bm{x}_{ij}|\bm{\mu}_{m})*P(\bm{\mu}_{m})}{\sum_{j=1}^{M}P(\bm{x}_{ij}|\bm{\mu}_{j})*P(\bm{\mu}_{j})}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.6" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(\bm{x}_{ij}|\bm{\mu}_{m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(\bm{x}_{ij}|\bm{\mu}_{m})</annotation></semantics></math> represents likelihood, and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(\bm{\mu}_{m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(\bm{\mu}_{m})</annotation></semantics></math> is the probability of each cached information. Generally, it is assumed a uniform prior over all <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mu}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mu}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e. <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(\bm{\mu}_{m})=\frac{1}{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>M</mi></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(\bm{\mu}_{m})=\frac{1}{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, reflecting no preference in the absence of additional information.
By substituting Eq. (<a href="#S4.E5" title="In IV-A1 Analysis ‣ IV-A Bayesian Inference for Cache-Based Predictions ‣ IV Method ‣ Bayesian Test-time Adaptation for Object Recognition and Detection with Vision-language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(\bm{\mu}_{m})=\frac{1}{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>M</mi></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(\bm{\mu}_{m})=\frac{1}{M}</annotation></semantics></math> back into Eq. (<a href="#S4.E4" title="In IV-A1 Analysis ‣ IV-A Bayesian Inference for Cache-Based Predictions ‣ IV Method ‣ Bayesian Test-time Adaptation for Object Recognition and Detection with Vision-language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>), we can obtain:</p>
<table id="S4.E6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S4.E6X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E6X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P(Y|\bm{x}_{ij})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P(Y|\bm{x}_{ij})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E6X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sum_{m=1}^{M}\frac{P(\bm{x}_{ij}|\bm{\mu}_{m})}{\sum_{j=1}^{M}P(\bm{x}_{ij}|\bm{\mu}_{j})}*P(Y|\bm{\mu}_{m})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></munderover></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sum_{m=1}^{M}\frac{P(\bm{x}_{ij}|\bm{\mu}_{m})}{\sum_{j=1}^{M}P(\bm{x}_{ij}|\bm{\mu}_{j})}*P(Y|\bm{\mu}_{m}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark:</span> <span id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(1): Connection to VLMs:</span>
The formulation in Eq. (<a href="#S4.E6" title="In IV-A1 Analysis ‣ IV-A Bayesian Inference for Cache-Based Predictions ‣ IV Method ‣ Bayesian Test-time Adaptation for Object Recognition and Detection with Vision-language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) is general and can be applied to vision-language models, including CLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> and Grounding DINO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>. In both models, the number of class embeddings is set as <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=K</annotation></semantics></math>, and each class embedding is defined as <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mu}_{k}=\bm{f}_{k}^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mu}_{k}=\bm{f}_{k}^{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. For the likelihood <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(\bm{x}_{ij}|\bm{\mu}_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(\bm{x}_{ij}|\bm{\mu}_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>, CLIP uses cosine similarity between the visual embedding <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{f}_{ij}^{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝒇</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>v</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{f}_{ij}^{v}</annotation></semantics></math> and cached embedding <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mu}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mu}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(\bm{x}_{ij}|\bm{\mu}_{k})\propto\exp(\cos(\bm{f}_{ij}^{v},\bm{\mu}_{k}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒇</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>v</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(\bm{x}_{ij}|\bm{\mu}_{k})\propto\exp(\cos(\bm{f}_{ij}^{v},\bm{\mu}_{k}))</annotation></semantics></math>. While Grounding DINO enhances this by applying a sigmoid activation function <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> to modulate the similarity scores: <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(\bm{x}_{ij}|\bm{\mu}_{k})\propto\exp(\sigma(\cos(\bm{f}_{ij}^{v},\bm{\mu}_{k})))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒇</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>v</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(\bm{x}_{ij}|\bm{\mu}_{k})\propto\exp(\sigma(\cos(\bm{f}_{ij}^{v},\bm{\mu}_{k})))</annotation></semantics></math>. For the prior <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(Y|\bm{\mu}_{k})\in\mathbb{R}^{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>K</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(Y|\bm{\mu}_{k})\in\mathbb{R}^{K}</annotation></semantics></math>, both CLIP and Grounding DINO initially use a fixed one-hot vector derived from labels, where the <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-th element is 1 and all other are 0. This means that when a sample belongs to the class embeddings <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mu}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mu}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, its probability of being in <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-th category is 1. By substituting these into Eq. (<a href="#S4.E6" title="In IV-A1 Analysis ‣ IV-A Bayesian Inference for Cache-Based Predictions ‣ IV Method ‣ Bayesian Test-time Adaptation for Object Recognition and Detection with Vision-language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>), we can obtain Eq. (<a href="#S3.E1" title="In III Preliminaries ‣ Bayesian Test-time Adaptation for Object Recognition and Detection with Vision-language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) for CLIP and Eq. (<a href="#S3.E2" title="In III Preliminaries ‣ Bayesian Test-time Adaptation for Object Recognition and Detection with Vision-language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) for Grounding DINO.
<span id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(2): Core Factors and Our Solution:</span> From Eq. (<a href="#S4.E6" title="In IV-A1 Analysis ‣ IV-A Bayesian Inference for Cache-Based Predictions ‣ IV Method ‣ Bayesian Test-time Adaptation for Object Recognition and Detection with Vision-language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>), we observe that computing the prediction <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(Y|\bm{x}_{ij})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(Y|\bm{x}_{ij})</annotation></semantics></math> hinges on two components: the likelihood <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(\bm{x}_{ij}|\bm{\mu}_{m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(\bm{x}_{ij}|\bm{\mu}_{m})</annotation></semantics></math>, which quantifies the alignment between the current proposal and cached entries <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mu}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mu}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the prior <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(Y|\bm{\mu}_{m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(Y|\bm{\mu}_{m})</annotation></semantics></math>, which captures the class distribution for each <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mu}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mu}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>. However, in VLMs, both <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mu}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mu}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(Y|\bm{\mu}_{m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(Y|\bm{\mu}_{m})</annotation></semantics></math> are fixed during inference. These fixed parameters are typically derived from pre-trained data, which may lead to a significant distribution shift when applied to test data with different characteristics. Such discrepancies can degrade model performance, particularly in open-vocabulary or cross-domain scenarios. To address this issue, we propose a dynamic caching mechanism that continuously updates both the likelihood and the prior based on incoming test data. By leveraging this dynamic cache-based approach, our method effectively bridges the train-test distribution gap, significantly improving model robustness and accuracy across diverse scenarios.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS2.6" class="ltx_text">IV-A</span>2 </span><span id="S4.SS1.SSS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Implementation</span></h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For the current <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th image <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, the pre-trained VLM extracts <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{(\bm{f}_{ij}^{v},\bm{p}^{init}_{ij})\}_{j=1}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒇</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>v</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒑</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(\bm{f}_{ij}^{v},\bm{p}^{init}_{ij})\}_{j=1}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> for recognition or high-confidence proposals <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{(\bm{f}_{ij}^{v},\bm{b}_{ij},\bm{p}^{init}_{ij})\}_{j=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒇</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>v</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒑</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(\bm{f}_{ij}^{v},\bm{b}_{ij},\bm{p}^{init}_{ij})\}_{j=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> for detection as mentioned earlier, which makes <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}_{ij}=(\bm{f}_{ij}^{v})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒇</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>v</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{ij}=(\bm{f}_{ij}^{v})</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}_{ij}=(\bm{f}_{ij}^{v},\bm{b}_{ij})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒇</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>v</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{ij}=(\bm{f}_{ij}^{v},\bm{b}_{ij})</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{p}^{init}_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝒑</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{p}^{init}_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> is computed using fixed text embedding and prior from the pre-trained VLM, it may exhibit a distribution shift when applied to test data. To address this issue, we construct a cache that stores information from the previous <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i-1</annotation></semantics></math> images to assist in prediction. The cache entries are defined as <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mu}_{m}=(\bm{f}_{m}^{cache})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mu}_{m}=(\bm{f}_{m}^{cache})</annotation></semantics></math> for recognition tasks and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mu}_{m}=(\bm{f}_{m}^{cache},\bm{b}_{m}^{cache})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mu}_{m}=(\bm{f}_{m}^{cache},\bm{b}_{m}^{cache})</annotation></semantics></math> for detection tasks. Here, <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{f}_{m}^{cache}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{f}_{m}^{cache}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the normalized mean of feature embeddings, while <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{b}_{m}^{cache}=[w,h]\in\mathbb{R}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{b}_{m}^{cache}=[w,h]\in\mathbb{R}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> aggregates the mean width and height of bounding boxes for similar spatial scales. These cached entries summarize historical information, enabling the model to adapt to the test data distribution dynamically. Importantly, the number of cached entries <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is adaptive and can expand during inference to incorporate new knowledge observed during test time. The specific update mechanism for <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> and the cached entries will be detailed in subsequent sections.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Eq. (<a href="#S4.E6" title="In IV-A1 Analysis ‣ IV-A Bayesian Inference for Cache-Based Predictions ‣ IV Method ‣ Bayesian Test-time Adaptation for Object Recognition and Detection with Vision-language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>), the evaluation of the likelihood <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(\bm{x}_{ij}|\bm{\mu}_{m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(\bm{x}_{ij}|\bm{\mu}_{m})</annotation></semantics></math> constitutes a critical step in deriving cache-based predictions, where <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the current observation from the VLM and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mu}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mu}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-th cache entry. To compute the likelihood, we incorporate two key components: feature similarity, which measures the alignment between the visual embedding <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{f}_{ij}^{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝒇</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>v</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{f}_{ij}^{v}</annotation></semantics></math> and the cached feature embedding <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{f}_{m}^{cache}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{f}_{m}^{cache}</annotation></semantics></math> for both recognition and detection tasks, and box similarity, which evaluates the spatial consistency between the bounding box <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{b}_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{b}_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> and the cached spatial scale <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{b}_{m}^{cache}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{b}_{m}^{cache}</annotation></semantics></math> for detection task.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Consistent with many prior methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>, the feature similarity is quantified as the cosine similarity between the current feature embedding <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{f}_{ij^{v}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>j</mi><mi>v</mi></msup></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{f}_{ij^{v}}</annotation></semantics></math> and the cached feature embedding <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{f}_{m}^{cache}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{f}_{m}^{cache}</annotation></semantics></math>, defined as:</p>
<table id="S4.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{jm}^{F}=\cos(\bm{f}_{ij}^{v},\bm{f}_{m}^{cache})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mi>F</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒇</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>v</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{jm}^{F}=\cos(\bm{f}_{ij}^{v},\bm{f}_{m}^{cache}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">For box similarity, we extract <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\bm{b}_{ij}[2:]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0em">:</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{b}_{ij}[2:]</annotation></semantics></math>, which contains the width and height <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[w,h]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[w,h]</annotation></semantics></math>, from the bounding box <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{b}_{ij}=[x,y,w,h]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{b}_{ij}=[x,y,w,h]</annotation></semantics></math> and compare them with the cached scale <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{b}_{m}^{cache}=[w,h]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{b}_{m}^{cache}=[w,h]</annotation></semantics></math>. The scale similarity is computed as:</p>
<table id="S4.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E8.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="S_{jm}^{B}=1-\frac{\|{\bm{b}_{ij}[2:]-\bm{b}_{m}^{cache}}\|}{\sqrt{2}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>B</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>𝒃</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0em">:</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>𝒃</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>m</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mfrac><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{jm}^{B}=1-\frac{\|{\bm{b}_{ij}[2:]-\bm{b}_{m}^{cache}}\|}{\sqrt{2}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\|\cdot\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∥</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\cdot\|</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the L2 norm and the difference is normalized by <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. This normalization arises because the ranges of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> are constrained to <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, resulting in a maximum difference of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.m12" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\|\bm{b}_{ij}[2:]-\bm{b}_{m}^{cache}\|=\sqrt{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0em">:</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">∥</mo><mo lspace="0.0835em">=</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\bm{b}_{ij}[2:]-\bm{b}_{m}^{cache}\|=\sqrt{2}</annotation></semantics></math> under perfect misalignment, thereby scaling the similarity score to the interval <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">For recognition tasks, where no bounding box information is available, the box similarity term <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{S}_{jm}^{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mi>B</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{S}_{jm}^{B}</annotation></semantics></math> is omitted, and the likelihood simplifies to:</p>
<table id="S4.E9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S4.E9X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E9X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P(\bm{x}_{ij}|\bm{\mu}_{m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P(\bm{x}_{ij}|\bm{\mu}_{m})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E9X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\propto\text{exp}({S}_{jm}^{F})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mtext>exp</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mi>F</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\propto\text{exp}({S}_{jm}^{F}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">For detection tasks, both terms are retained, ensuring that the likelihood reflects both semantic alignment and spatial consistency. This is achieved through a weighted combination of the box similarity <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{S}_{jm}^{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mi>B</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{S}_{jm}^{B}</annotation></semantics></math> and the feature similarity <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{S}_{jm}^{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mi>F</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{S}_{jm}^{F}</annotation></semantics></math>, expressed as:</p>
<table id="S4.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(\bm{x}_{ij}|\bm{\mu}_{m})\propto\text{exp}(w_{s}*{S}_{jm}^{B}+(1-w_{s})*{S}_{jm}^{F})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mtext>exp</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mi>B</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mi>F</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(\bm{x}_{ij}|\bm{\mu}_{m})\propto\text{exp}(w_{s}*{S}_{jm}^{B}+(1-w_{s})*{S}_{jm}^{F}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p5.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{s}\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{s}\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> is a balance hyperparameter, <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{exp}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>exp</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{exp}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the exponential function.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Beyond the likelihood computation outlined in Eq. (<a href="#S4.E6" title="In IV-A1 Analysis ‣ IV-A Bayesian Inference for Cache-Based Predictions ‣ IV Method ‣ Bayesian Test-time Adaptation for Object Recognition and Detection with Vision-language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>), it is imperative to consider the prior <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(Y|\bm{\mu}_{m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(Y|\bm{\mu}_{m})</annotation></semantics></math> to fully characterize the cache-based prediction. Conventional test-time adaptation methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> typically employ a fixed one-hot vector to represent this prior, assuming a static distribution across environments. However, this overlooks the dynamic nature of real-world scenarios; for instance, consider a person presenting with a fever—during the COVID-19 pandemic, the prior probability of COVID-19 should be significantly higher, whereas in a typical season, a common cold might be more probable. Therefore, we propose a dynamic prior by maintaining a set of vectors <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(Y|\bm{\mu}_{m})=\bm{v}^{cache}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒗</mi><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(Y|\bm{\mu}_{m})=\bm{v}^{cache}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{v}^{cache}_{m}\in\mathbb{R}^{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒗</mi><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>K</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{v}^{cache}_{m}\in\mathbb{R}^{K}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the probability distribution over <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> categories for each <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mu}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mu}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>. Unlike the fixed one-hot distribution used in previous methods, our approach adaptively learns priors through continuous updates of <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{v}^{cache}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝒗</mi><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{v}^{cache}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> based on the observed samples. The specific update mechanism for these priors will be detailed in section <a href="#S4.SS3" title="IV-C Cache Adaptation ‣ IV Method ‣ Bayesian Test-time Adaptation for Object Recognition and Detection with Vision-language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV-C</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p7.3" class="ltx_p">To improve computational efficiency and scalability, we implement the cache-based prediction in matrix form. The cached feature embeddings are concatenated into a matrix <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{F}_{cache}=[\bm{f}_{1}^{cache};\cdots;\bm{f}_{M}^{cache}]\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑭</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒇</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>;</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>;</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{F}_{cache}=[\bm{f}_{1}^{cache};\cdots;\bm{f}_{M}^{cache}]\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times M}</annotation></semantics></math>, the cached spatial scales into <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{B}_{cache}=[\bm{b}_{1}^{cache};\cdots;\bm{b}_{M}^{cache}]\in\mathbb{R}^{2\times M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑩</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒃</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>;</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>;</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒃</mi><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{B}_{cache}=[\bm{b}_{1}^{cache};\cdots;\bm{b}_{M}^{cache}]\in\mathbb{R}^{2\times M}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the cached priors into <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{V}_{cache}=[\bm{v}_{1}^{cache};\cdots;\bm{v}_{M}^{cache}]\in\mathbb{R}^{K\times M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑽</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒗</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>;</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>;</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒗</mi><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{V}_{cache}=[\bm{v}_{1}^{cache};\cdots;\bm{v}_{M}^{cache}]\in\mathbb{R}^{K\times M}</annotation></semantics></math>. Based on Eq. (<a href="#S4.E9" title="In IV-A2 Implementation ‣ IV-A Bayesian Inference for Cache-Based Predictions ‣ IV Method ‣ Bayesian Test-time Adaptation for Object Recognition and Detection with Vision-language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>), Eq. (<a href="#S4.E10" title="In IV-A2 Implementation ‣ IV-A Bayesian Inference for Cache-Based Predictions ‣ IV Method ‣ Bayesian Test-time Adaptation for Object Recognition and Detection with Vision-language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>) and Eq. (<a href="#S4.E4" title="In IV-A1 Analysis ‣ IV-A Bayesian Inference for Cache-Based Predictions ‣ IV Method ‣ Bayesian Test-time Adaptation for Object Recognition and Detection with Vision-language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>), the <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(\bm{U}|\bm{x}_{ij})=[P(\bm{\mu}_{1}|\bm{x}_{ij}),\cdots,P(\bm{\mu}_{M}|\bm{x}_{ij})]\in\mathbb{R}^{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>M</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(\bm{U}|\bm{x}_{ij})=[P(\bm{\mu}_{1}|\bm{x}_{ij}),\cdots,P(\bm{\mu}_{M}|\bm{x}_{ij})]\in\mathbb{R}^{M}</annotation></semantics></math> for object recognition is formulated as:</p>
<table id="S4.E11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S4.E11X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E11X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P(\bm{U}|\bm{x}_{ij})=\text{Softmax}({S}_{j}^{F})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>Softmax</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>F</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P(\bm{U}|\bm{x}_{ij})=\text{Softmax}({S}_{j}^{F}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p7.4" class="ltx_p">and the <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(\bm{U}|\bm{x}_{ij})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(\bm{U}|\bm{x}_{ij})</annotation></semantics></math> for object detection is formulated as:</p>
<table id="S4.E12" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S4.E12X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E12X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P(\bm{U}|\bm{x}_{ij})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P(\bm{U}|\bm{x}_{ij})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E12X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\text{Softmax}(w_{s}*{S}_{j}^{B}+(1-w_{s})*{S}_{j}^{F})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>Softmax</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>B</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>F</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\text{Softmax}(w_{s}*{S}_{j}^{B}+(1-w_{s})*{S}_{j}^{F}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p7.5" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Softmax}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Softmax</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Softmax}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the softmax function,
the feature similarity vector <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{S}_{j}^{F}\in\mathbb{R}^{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>F</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>M</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{S}_{j}^{F}\in\mathbb{R}^{M}</annotation></semantics></math> is computed as <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{S}_{j}^{F}=\cos(\bm{f}_{ij}^{v},\bm{F}_{cache})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>F</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒇</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>v</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝑭</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{S}_{j}^{F}=\cos(\bm{f}_{ij}^{v},\bm{F}_{cache})</annotation></semantics></math>, and the box similarity vector <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{S}_{j}^{B}\in\mathbb{R}^{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>j</mi><mi>B</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>M</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{S}_{j}^{B}\in\mathbb{R}^{M}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p7.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{S}_{j}^{B}=1-\frac{\|\bm{b}_{ij}[2:]-\bm{B}_{cache}\|_{2}}{\sqrt{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>B</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>𝒃</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0em">:</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>𝑩</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em">∥</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{S}_{j}^{B}=1-\frac{\|\bm{b}_{ij}[2:]-\bm{B}_{cache}\|_{2}}{\sqrt{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p7.m11" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\|\cdot\|_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∥</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em">∥</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\cdot\|_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> denoting the L2 norm of matrix. Further, we derive the category-level posterior <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(Y|\bm{x}_{ij})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(Y|\bm{x}_{ij})</annotation></semantics></math> to capture the probabilistic distribution over <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> categories, which is formulated as:</p>
<table id="S4.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(Y|\bm{x}_{ij})=P(\bm{U}|\bm{x}_{ij})*\bm{V}_{cache}^{\top}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><msubsup><mi>𝑽</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(Y|\bm{x}_{ij})=P(\bm{U}|\bm{x}_{ij})*\bm{V}_{cache}^{\top}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p7.6" class="ltx_p">This computed <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p7.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(Y|\bm{x}_{ij})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(Y|\bm{x}_{ij})</annotation></semantics></math> represents the cache-based probability <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p7.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{p}^{cache}_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝒑</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{p}^{cache}_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math>, integrating both adaptive likelihoods by updating <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p7.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\bm{\mu}_{m}\}_{i=1}^{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\bm{\mu}_{m}\}_{i=1}^{M}</annotation></semantics></math> and priors by updating <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p7.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\bm{v}^{cache}_{m}\}_{i=1}^{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒗</mi><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\bm{v}^{cache}_{m}\}_{i=1}^{M}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">IV-B</span> </span><span id="S4.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Uncertainty-Guided Fusion</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">After processing through the cache, we compute a cache-based prediction <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{p}^{cache}_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝒑</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{p}^{cache}_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> for both recognition and detection tasks. This prediction leverages historical information stored in the cache, capturing both adaptive likelihoods and priors. To further enhance robustness, we fuse <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{p}^{cache}_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝒑</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{p}^{cache}_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> with the initial prediction <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{p}^{init}_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝒑</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{p}^{init}_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> computed by the pre-trained VLM. The fusion is performed using an entropy-based uncertainty weighting mechanism, which dynamically balances the contributions of <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{p}^{init}_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝒑</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{p}^{init}_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{p}^{cache}_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝒑</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{p}^{cache}_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> based on their respective uncertainties. The final prediction <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{p}^{final}_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝒑</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{p}^{final}_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> is formulated as:</p>
<table id="S4.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{p}^{final}_{ij}=\frac{\exp(-\text{E}(\bm{p}^{init}_{ij}))\cdot\bm{p}^{init}_{ij}+\exp(-\text{E}(\bm{p}^{cache}_{ij}))\cdot\bm{p}^{cache}_{ij}}{\exp(-\text{E}(\bm{p}^{init}_{ij}))+\exp(-\text{E}(\bm{p}^{cache}_{ij}))}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒑</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mtext>E</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒑</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒑</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mtext>E</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒑</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒑</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mtext>E</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒑</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mtext>E</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒑</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{p}^{final}_{ij}=\frac{\exp(-\text{E}(\bm{p}^{init}_{ij}))\cdot\bm{p}^{init}_{ij}+\exp(-\text{E}(\bm{p}^{cache}_{ij}))\cdot\bm{p}^{cache}_{ij}}{\exp(-\text{E}(\bm{p}^{init}_{ij}))+\exp(-\text{E}(\bm{p}^{cache}_{ij}))},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where the Shannon entropy is defined as <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\text{E}(\bm{p})=-\sum_{k=1}^{K}p_{k}\log p_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>E</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒑</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.055em">−</mo><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{E}(\bm{p})=-\sum_{k=1}^{K}p_{k}\log p_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. By combining <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{p}^{init}_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝒑</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{p}^{init}_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{p}^{cache}_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝒑</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{p}^{cache}_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> in this manner, the resulting <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{p}^{final}_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝒑</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{p}^{final}_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> achieves better robustness and generalization.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">IV-C</span> </span><span id="S4.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Cache Adaptation</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The cache, consisting of <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{F}_{cache}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑭</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{F}_{cache}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{B}_{cache}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑩</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{B}_{cache}</annotation></semantics></math> (for detection tasks), and <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{V}_{cache}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑽</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{V}_{cache}</annotation></semantics></math>, plays a crucial role in supporting predictions for the test domain. To ensure the cache remains representative of the evolving test data distribution, it is dynamically updated as new test samples arrive. This continuous updating mechanism enables the cache to capture the characteristics of the test domain more accurately, thereby improving the model’s ability to generate precise predictions for subsequent samples. Specifically, after predicting the current sample <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, the cache is updated using the final prediction <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{p}^{final}_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝒑</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{p}^{final}_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> derived from <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. For recognition tasks, this takes the form of <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{(\bm{f}^{v}_{ij},\bm{p}^{final}_{ij})\}_{j=1}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒇</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>v</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒑</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(\bm{f}^{v}_{ij},\bm{p}^{final}_{ij})\}_{j=1}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, while for detection tasks, it is <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{(\bm{f}^{v}_{ij},\bm{b}_{ij},\bm{p}^{final}_{ij})\}_{j=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒇</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>v</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒑</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(\bm{f}^{v}_{ij},\bm{b}_{ij},\bm{p}^{final}_{ij})\}_{j=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>. To ensure that only reliable information contributes to the cache, we filter out low-confidence proposals by retaining those that satisfy:
<math id="S4.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max_{k}p^{final}_{ijk}\geq\tau_{1}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max_{k}p^{final}_{ijk}\geq\tau_{1},</annotation></semantics></math>
where <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is a confidence threshold. Only the remaining high-confidence proposals are used to update the cache.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For each retained proposal <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math>, we utilize its pre-computed matching distribution <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(\bm{U}|\bm{x}_{ij})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(\bm{U}|\bm{x}_{ij})</annotation></semantics></math>, derived during prediction using Eq. (<a href="#S4.E11" title="In IV-A2 Implementation ‣ IV-A Bayesian Inference for Cache-Based Predictions ‣ IV Method ‣ Bayesian Test-time Adaptation for Object Recognition and Detection with Vision-language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>) or Eq. (<a href="#S4.E12" title="In IV-A2 Implementation ‣ IV-A Bayesian Inference for Cache-Based Predictions ‣ IV Method ‣ Bayesian Test-time Adaptation for Object Recognition and Detection with Vision-language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>), to identify the most similar cache entry. Specifically, the matching score for each cache entry is defined as <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(\bm{U}|\bm{x}_{ij})[m]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(\bm{U}|\bm{x}_{ij})[m]</annotation></semantics></math>, and the best-matching entry is selected as:</p>
<table id="S4.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m^{*}=\arg\max_{m}P(\bm{U}|\bm{x}_{ij})[m]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><munder><mi>max</mi><mi>m</mi></munder></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m^{*}=\arg\max_{m}P(\bm{U}|\bm{x}_{ij})[m].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(\bm{U}|\bm{x}_{ij})[m^{*}]&lt;\tau_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(\bm{U}|\bm{x}_{ij})[m^{*}]&lt;\tau_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is a similarity threshold, or if the cache is still empty, we create a new cache entry by appending the current feature, spatial scale (for detection tasks only and thus omitted by recognition tasks), and class distribution:</p>
<table id="S4.E16" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S4.E16X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E16X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bm{F}^{cache}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝑭</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bm{F}^{cache}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E16X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\bm{F}^{cache}\oplus\bm{f}_{ij}^{v}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝑭</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msup><mo>⊕</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒇</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>v</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\bm{F}^{cache}\oplus\bm{f}_{ij}^{v},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="4" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E16Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E16Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bm{B}^{cache}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝑩</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bm{B}^{cache}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E16Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\bm{B}^{cache}\oplus\bm{b}_{ij}[2:]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝑩</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msup><mo>⊕</mo><mi>𝒃</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0em">:</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\bm{B}^{cache}\oplus\bm{b}_{ij}[2:],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E16Xb" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E16Xb.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bm{V}^{cache}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝑽</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bm{V}^{cache}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E16Xb.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\bm{V}^{cache}\oplus\bm{p}^{final}_{ij}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝑽</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msup><mo>⊕</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒑</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\bm{V}^{cache}\oplus\bm{p}^{final}_{ij},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E16Xc" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E16Xc.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bm{C}^{cache}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝑪</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bm{C}^{cache}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E16Xc.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\bm{C}^{cache}\oplus 1," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝑪</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msup><mo>⊕</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\bm{C}^{cache}\oplus 1,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\oplus" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊕</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\oplus</annotation></semantics></math> denotes concatenation along the first dimension. Here, <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{C}^{cache}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝑪</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{C}^{cache}</annotation></semantics></math> tracks the number of updates for each cache entry, initialized as 1 for newly added entries.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p4.2" class="ltx_p">Otherwise, if <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(\bm{U}|\bm{x}_{ij})[m^{*}]\geq\tau_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(\bm{U}|\bm{x}_{ij})[m^{*}]\geq\tau_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, indicating that a sufficiently similar cache entry exists, we update the matched entry <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mu}_{m^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mu}_{m^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math> using a statistical aggregation method. Specifically, for both recognition and detection tasks, we update the feature embeddings and priors. Additionally, for the detection task, we also update the spatial scales:</p>
<table id="S4.E17" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S4.E17X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E17X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bm{f}_{m^{*}}^{cache}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝒇</mi><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bm{f}_{m^{*}}^{cache}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E17X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{c_{m^{*}}^{cache}\cdot\bm{f}_{m^{*}}^{cache}+\bm{f}_{ij}^{v}}{c_{m^{*}}^{cache}+1}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>c</mi><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒇</mi><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒇</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>v</mi></msubsup></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>c</mi><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{c_{m^{*}}^{cache}\cdot\bm{f}_{m^{*}}^{cache}+\bm{f}_{ij}^{v}}{c_{m^{*}}^{cache}+1},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="4" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E17Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E17Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bm{b}_{m^{*}}^{cache}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝒃</mi><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bm{b}_{m^{*}}^{cache}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E17Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{c_{m^{*}}^{cache}\cdot\bm{b}_{m^{*}}^{cache}+\bm{b}_{ij}[2:]}{c_{m^{*}}^{cache}+1}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>c</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>𝒃</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>𝒃</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0em">:</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>c</mi><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{c_{m^{*}}^{cache}\cdot\bm{b}_{m^{*}}^{cache}+\bm{b}_{ij}[2:]}{c_{m^{*}}^{cache}+1},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E17Xb" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E17Xb.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bm{v}_{m^{*}}^{cache}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝒗</mi><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bm{v}_{m^{*}}^{cache}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E17Xb.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{c_{m^{*}}^{cache}\cdot\bm{v}_{m^{*}}^{cache}+\bm{p}^{final}_{ij}}{c_{m^{*}}^{cache}+1}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>c</mi><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒗</mi><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒑</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>c</mi><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{c_{m^{*}}^{cache}\cdot\bm{v}_{m^{*}}^{cache}+\bm{p}^{final}_{ij}}{c_{m^{*}}^{cache}+1},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E17Xc" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E17Xc.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle c_{m^{*}}^{cache}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>c</mi><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle c_{m^{*}}^{cache}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E17Xc.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=c_{m^{*}}^{cache}+1." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>c</mi><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=c_{m^{*}}^{cache}+1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">This updating mechanism ensures that each cache entry evolves in a confidence-aware and data-driven manner. This dynamic adaptation enables our method to progressively refine its internal model of the test domain without requiring access to training data or parameter updates.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In summary, we have presented BCA+, a unified Bayesian framework for test-time adaptation in both object recognition and detection, which is summarized in Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ IV-C Cache Adaptation ‣ IV Method ‣ Bayesian Test-time Adaptation for Object Recognition and Detection with Vision-language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. By dynamically maintaining a cache of class embeddings, spatial scales, and adaptive priors, our method leverages historical information to refine predictions for incoming samples. The key innovation lies in its ability to seamlessly integrate the strengths of vision-language models with a robust, data-driven adaptation mechanism. Crucially, BCA+ is a training-free method that requires no backpropagation during inference. This design eliminates the computational bottleneck of gradient-based optimization, resulting in significantly reduced inference time and memory usage. In the next section, we will demonstrate that BCA+ not only achieves superior accuracy but also offers exceptional efficiency, making it a highly practical solution for real-world applications.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">

<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> Bayesian Test-Time Adaptive Object Recognition and Detection (BCA+)</figcaption><div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<p id="alg1.4" class="ltx_p ltx_figure_panel ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom"><span id="alg1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input</span>: A pre-trained VLM (CLIP or Grounding DINO), unlabeled test data <math id="alg1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{(\bm{x}_{i})\}_{i=1}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(\bm{x}_{i})\}_{i=1}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, hyperparameters <math id="alg1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="alg1.4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Procedure</span>:</p></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<div id="alg1.5" class="ltx_listing ltx_figure_panel ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
              
              
             Initialize the cache: <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{F}^{cache}=\varnothing" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝑭</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{F}^{cache}=\varnothing</annotation></semantics></math>; <math id="alg1.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{V}^{cache}=\varnothing" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝑽</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{V}^{cache}=\varnothing</annotation></semantics></math>; <math id="alg1.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{C}^{cache}=\varnothing" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝑪</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{C}^{cache}=\varnothing</annotation></semantics></math>; <math id="alg1.l1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{B}^{cache}=\varnothing" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝑩</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{B}^{cache}=\varnothing</annotation></semantics></math> (for detection) ;

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
              
              
             <span id="alg1.l2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> = 1:<math id="alg1.l2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>



</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
              
              
              Process <math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> with VLM to obtain initial outputs as <math id="alg1.l3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{(\bm{f}_{ij}^{v},\bm{p}^{init}_{ij})\}_{j=1}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒇</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>v</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒑</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(\bm{f}_{ij}^{v},\bm{p}^{init}_{ij})\}_{j=1}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> (recognition) or <math id="alg1.l3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{(\bm{f}_{ij}^{v},\bm{b}_{ij},\bm{p}^{init}_{ij})\}_{j=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒇</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mi>v</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒃</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒑</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(\bm{f}_{ij}^{v},\bm{b}_{ij},\bm{p}^{init}_{ij})\}_{j=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> (detection);

</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
              
              
              <span id="alg1.l4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> each proposal <math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>



</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
              
              
               Calculate <math id="alg1.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(\bm{U}|\bm{x}_{ij})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(\bm{U}|\bm{x}_{ij})</annotation></semantics></math> using Eq. (<a href="#S4.E11" title="In IV-A2 Implementation ‣ IV-A Bayesian Inference for Cache-Based Predictions ‣ IV Method ‣ Bayesian Test-time Adaptation for Object Recognition and Detection with Vision-language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>) (recognition) or Eq. (<a href="#S4.E12" title="In IV-A2 Implementation ‣ IV-A Bayesian Inference for Cache-Based Predictions ‣ IV Method ‣ Bayesian Test-time Adaptation for Object Recognition and Detection with Vision-language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>) (detection);

</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
              
              
               Calculate <math id="alg1.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{p}^{cache}_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝒑</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{p}^{cache}_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> using Eq. (<a href="#S4.E13" title="In IV-A2 Implementation ‣ IV-A Bayesian Inference for Cache-Based Predictions ‣ IV Method ‣ Bayesian Test-time Adaptation for Object Recognition and Detection with Vision-language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>);

</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">7:</span></span>
              
              
               Perform uncertainty-guided fusion based on <math id="alg1.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{p}^{init}_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝒑</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{p}^{init}_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="alg1.l7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{p}^{cache}_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝒑</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{p}^{cache}_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> to obtain <math id="alg1.l7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{p}^{final}_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝒑</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{p}^{final}_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> using Eq. (<a href="#S4.E14" title="In IV-B Uncertainty-Guided Fusion ‣ IV Method ‣ Bayesian Test-time Adaptation for Object Recognition and Detection with Vision-language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>);

</div>
<div id="alg1.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">8:</span></span>
              
              
              <span id="alg1.l8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l8.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l9.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">9:</span></span>
              
              
              <span id="alg1.l9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> each <math id="alg1.l9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{x}_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{x}_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="alg1.l9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max_{k}p^{final}_{ijk}\geq\tau_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max_{k}p^{final}_{ijk}\geq\tau_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l9.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>



</div>
<div id="alg1.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l10.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">10:</span></span>
              
              
               Find best-matching <math id="alg1.l10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>-th entry in the cache using Eq. (<a href="#S4.E15" title="In IV-C Cache Adaptation ‣ IV Method ‣ Bayesian Test-time Adaptation for Object Recognition and Detection with Vision-language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>);

</div>
<div id="alg1.l11" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l11.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">11:</span></span>
              
              
               <span id="alg1.l11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span> <math id="alg1.l11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(\bm{U}|\bm{x}_{ij})[m^{*}]&lt;\tau_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(\bm{U}|\bm{x}_{ij})[m^{*}]&lt;\tau_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> or cache is empty <span id="alg1.l11.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">then</span>



</div>
<div id="alg1.l12" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l12.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">12:</span></span>
              
              
                Create new entry using Eq. (<a href="#S4.E16" title="In IV-C Cache Adaptation ‣ IV Method ‣ Bayesian Test-time Adaptation for Object Recognition and Detection with Vision-language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>);

</div>
<div id="alg1.l13" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l13.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">13:</span></span>
              
              
               <span id="alg1.l13.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">else</span>


</div>
<div id="alg1.l14" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l14.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">14:</span></span>
              
              
                Update matched entry <math id="alg1.l14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\mu}_{m^{*}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝝁</mi><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\mu}_{m^{*}}</annotation></semantics></math> using Eq. (<a href="#S4.E17" title="In IV-C Cache Adaptation ‣ IV Method ‣ Bayesian Test-time Adaptation for Object Recognition and Detection with Vision-language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>);

</div>
<div id="alg1.l15" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l15.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">15:</span></span>
              
              
               <span id="alg1.l15.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l15.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l16" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l16.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">16:</span></span>
              
              
              <span id="alg1.l16.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l16.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l17" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l17.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">17:</span></span>
              
              
              <span id="alg1.l17.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span> final predictions <math id="alg1.l17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\bm{p}^{final}_{ij}\}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒑</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\bm{p}^{final}_{ij}\}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l18" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l18.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">18:</span></span>
              
              
             <span id="alg1.l18.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l18.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
</div></div></div>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">V </span><span id="S5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Experiments</span></h2>

<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">V-A</span> </span><span id="S5.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Experimental Setup</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Datasets: </span> We evaluate our method BCA+ on standard benchmarks for both object recognition and object detection to demonstrate its effectiveness and robustness under various distribution shifts.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For object recognition, we use two established benchmarks: the Out-of-Distribution (OOD) benchmark and the Cross-Domain benchmark. The OOD benchmark evaluates robustness to data that significantly differs from the training distribution. It uses four challenging datasets derived from ImageNet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>: ImageNet-A <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>]</cite>, ImageNet-V2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">55</a>]</cite>, ImageNet-R <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>]</cite>, and ImageNet-S <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">57</a>]</cite>, which contain adversarial, re-sampled, and stylized images, respectively.
The Cross-Domain benchmark assesses a model’s generalization across diverse image domains. It comprises ten datasets: Aircraft <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">58</a>]</cite>, Caltech101 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>]</cite>, Cars <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">60</a>]</cite>, DTD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">61</a>]</cite>, EuroSAT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">62</a>]</cite>, Flower102 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a>]</cite>, Food101 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">64</a>]</cite>, Pets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">65</a>]</cite>, SUN397 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">66</a>]</cite>, and UCF101 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">67</a>]</cite>. Each dataset represents a distinct visual domain, providing a comprehensive evaluation of a model’s adaptability to new tasks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For object detection, we evaluate our method on three datasets designed to simulate real-world distribution shifts through image corruption following previous works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>. Specifically, we employ three corrupted datasets: (1) FoggyCityscapes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">68</a>]</cite>, a synthetic foggy version of Cityscapes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">69</a>]</cite>, where we test on the densest fog level (<math id="S5.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=0.02" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.02</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=0.02</annotation></semantics></math>);(2) PASCAL-C, a corrupted version of the PASCAL VOC 2007 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">70</a>]</cite> test set (4,952 images) with 15 corruption types <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">71</a>]</cite> at severity 5; (3) COCO-C, which applies the same corruptions to 5,000 images from the MS-COCO 2017 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> validation set.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">TABLE I: </span>Object Recognition Performance Comparison with the State-of-the-Art Methods on the OOD Benchmark. 
<br class="ltx_break">Metric: classification accuracy (%);
Bp-free: backpropagation-free at test time;
Average: mean accuracy across all datasets;
OOD Average: mean accuracy across four OOD datasets excluding ImageNet.</figcaption>
